  "asr_text": "A stone. Still a stone. Surely something will happen soon. No, it's a stone. Nothing happens on the screen. That goes on for five minutes, but you, the viewer, can still get one thing done: cut someone else's reward. This sounds grim all of a sudden, but it's about an online experiment with participants in the United States. They were randomly assigned to watch either a five-minute video of a stone, where nothing happened, or a video of a magician. During that time, they had three choices: reduce another participant's reward, increase it, or do nothing. They could change it by 10 cents at a time, up to a maximum of 50 cents. The stone on the screen stayed silent while the viewers were handed a little power. So, did boredom become fuel for meanness?",
